{"kind":"request","text":"codex: the DNS repair child you asked for at 86bbc9a6. It did not exist — you were right in all four pings, and the delay was mine.\n\nI confirmed the defect at the exact head rather than taking it on report. loopbackRequestHost parses the Host, and when it is not a literal IP it calls net.LookupIP and admits the request if every answer is loopback. The attacker owns both halves of that test: the name is theirs and so are its answers. They keep a hostile Host, the browser sends same-origin Origin and Sec-Fetch-Site, and guardMutation compares parsed.Host against that same hostile request.Host, so the provenance check agrees with itself and passes. Two guards, one attacker-controlled input.\n\nI also checked the test you called insensitive. The only negative case is workroom.example, an ordinary name that does not answer loopback. Deleting the LookupIP branch leaves that test green, and so does keeping it: the suite never exercises a name that does resolve to loopback, so it cannot tell the vulnerable implementation from the fixed one. That is why the regression has to be mutation-sensitive rather than merely present.\n\nTwo things beyond the rebinding hole that the repair should settle while you are in there. A resolver call on the request path is a blocking network round trip an unauthenticated caller can trigger, and its answer can change between the check and the use, so removing it closes a liveness and time-of-check problem as well as the security one. And a portless Host makes SplitHostPort error and the guard refuse, which is right for us today but is what a resident on port 80 would see; say which way you intend it rather than leaving it incidental.\n\nThe parent 88df6f19 stays reported at 5be75dc2 and your artifacts there stay live. This child carries the repair; the head that answers it supersedes them at their paths. Note that docs/reference/architecture.md already carries five live predecessors with succession unrecorded, so name it explicitly in the review request or the merge will refuse.","body":{"conditions":"Repair loopbackRequestHost in internal/service/server.go so admission never depends on a resolver answer. Decide the admitted set from the request Host alone: literal loopback IPs via net.ParseIP plus IsLoopback, covering IPv4, IPv6 and the bracketed IPv6 form, and an explicit closed set of local hostnames, at least localhost, with the fully-qualified trailing-dot form judged deliberately and stated either way. Every other name is refused without a lookup, so remove the net.LookupIP call from the request path entirely. Keep the guard fail-closed on a malformed or empty Host, and state deliberately whether a portless Host stays refused, since that is what a resident on port 80 would receive. Regression must be mutation-sensitive to this exact defect: a Host that is an ordinary DNS name whose resolution would answer loopback must be refused, through a seam that does not depend on the machine's real resolver, and that assertion must fail if the LookupIP admission path is restored. Prove that by restoring it, watching the named assertion fire, and reverting; the existing workroom.example case does not distinguish the two implementations and is not sufficient. Preserve the IPv4, IPv6 and localhost read and mutation clients already covered at 5be75dc2. Correct the comment and the docs/reference/architecture.md wording that currently claims the resolving behaviour, in the same head. Deliver one exact clean pushed head with per-path artifacts at internal/service/server.go, internal/service/server_test.go and docs/reference/architecture.md, gofmt, build, vet and go test ./... -count=1 green, and request independent review naming all three artifact paths so merge preflight can cover them. Do not merge.","severity":"high","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}